title five births, and the onboarding that never landed
prompt go -- run the births; and the jail display issue is back
think Five trees born in 88 seconds, every one GREEN with remotes, hooks, submodules, its own rye and rishi, and a witness proven inside it.
think This was the first real test of the birth repair from %427, and it held: 8 trees, 36 paths, zero foreign. Copal signs from its own keyring and reaches xy from its own deploy key, with both config values naming its own tree.
think The display fault is not the mount. %408 armed that and the file exists at 39 KB -- but it held 28 keys, every one a cache, and no onboarding marker at all, while the host's file held 50 including hasCompletedOnboarding and lastOnboardingVersion. So every jailed launch re-ran onboarding, which is where the unreadable text lives.
obs The elder comment argued its case well and I kept the argument while replacing the conclusion. It said a hand-written marker would be a guess at another program's schema, and that Claude writes its own record on the first jailed run. The first half stands; the second is disproven by the file itself. Copying two keys the host's own Claude wrote is observation rather than invention, and only those two are carried -- theme, auth, and every cache stay the jailed session's own.
obs When the host file is absent the elder behaviour stands, because a pier with no record has nothing to copy.
loom births count=5 wall_s=88 all_green=yes
loom key_locality trees=8 paths=36 foreign=0
file tools/ag/agent-jail.sh the host's onboarding markers are carried into the jailed state
recommend check-in Keaton -- relaunch the jail and read whether the onboarding text is gone; the access question wants his ruling
